# Line endings, decided once, so that a byte is the same byte everywhere.
#
# ## WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, and it is two measured symptoms rather than tidiness
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on this machine and there was no `.gitattributes`, so
# git stored LF, checked out CRLF, and **every text file in this repository had
# two different sha256s** - one for the bytes git holds, one for the bytes on
# disk. Two things fell out of that, both measured on 2026-09-10/11:
#
# 1. **A release build dirtied a tracked file and blocked the next push.**
#    `tauri build` rewrote `src-tauri/Cargo.toml`'s line endings with no content
#    change, `git status` reported it modified, and `push_if_green` refused with
#    exit 2 - "a gate would certify a tree that is not the object this would
#    push". The refusal was correct. The cause was this.
#
# 2. **Three published checksums could not be verified on this platform.**
#    `evals/architecture-json/held-out.sha256` and its two siblings record the
#    hash of the LF form. Checked against the CRLF file on disk they MISMATCH -
#    all three, today, before this file existed. Normalising does not break
#    them; it is what makes them checkable at all.
#
# A hash that does not say which bytes it hashed cannot be checked, and the
# honest fix is not to label every hash twice forever - it is to stop having two
# answers.
#
# ## WHAT `text=auto eol=lf` DOES
#
# Git decides what is text; everything it calls text is stored AND checked out
# with LF, on every platform. Windows editors have handled LF for many years,
# and the two symptoms above are what the alternative costs.

* text=auto eol=lf

# ## THE BINARIES, MARKED EXPLICITLY
#
# `text=auto` already detects these by content, and marking them is belt and
# braces: a detection heuristic that guesses wrong on one file corrupts it
# silently, and a corrupted PNG is not a thing anybody notices in a diff.
#
# The first two are what this repository actually tracks (166 `.png`, 1 `.ico`,
# counted). The rest are formats this project produces or fetches - adapters,
# wheels, sidecars, models - which are gitignored today and would arrive
# unannounced the first time one is not.

*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
*.msi   binary
*.whl   binary
*.safetensors binary
*.gguf  binary
*.bin   binary
*.onnx  binary
*.npy   binary
*.npz   binary

# Shell scripts that a POSIX shell runs need LF whatever a Windows editor
# prefers; `eol=lf` above already gives them that, and saying so here is for the
# reader rather than for git.
*.sh    text eol=lf
